course: language
lesson: 19
title: Accents and dialects around the world
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: English is spoken all over the world, and every place has grown its own accent and words: a fizzy drink is "pop" in much of the American Midwest, "soda" in the Northeast and California, and sometimes just "coke" in parts of the South.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different countries: in Cape Town "Turn left at the robot" means at the traffic light, and in Sydney "Grab your togs and thongs" means your swimsuit and flip-flops.
explain: Some Englishes have grown into rich systems of their own: Indian English gives us "prepone" (move earlier, the opposite of postpone), Singapore's Singlish says "Can or not?" and "Can lah!", and Jamaican Patois grew from English mixed with West African languages.
explain: Accents grow from the people who settle a place: many of Australia's first settlers in the late 1700s and 1800s came from London, south-east England and Ireland, and their mix became the Australian accent.
explain: None of these is "broken English"; each has its own rules, and a smart listener uses the situation to work out new words, then asks.
fact: In Pittsburgh "yinz" means you all; a fizzy drink is "pop" in much of the Midwest, "soda" in the Northeast and California, and sometimes "coke" in parts of the South.
fact: In Australia "togs" means swimsuit, "thongs" means flip-flops and "arvo" means afternoon; in other countries "thongs" can mean something different, which causes funny mix-ups.
fact: In New Zealand "sweet as" means great or no problem, with nothing after "as", and "chur" means thanks or cool, influenced by Māori English.
fact: In South African English a "robot" is a traffic light, "lekker" means great or tasty (from Afrikaans), "now now" means soon and "just now" means some time later.
fact: In Indian English "do the needful" means do what is needed, "revert" often means reply, and "prepone" means move to an earlier time; Indian English is spoken by many millions.
fact: In Singapore English (Singlish) "Can or not?" means is that possible?, "Can lah!" means yes, sure, and "lah" adds feeling; Singlish mixes English with Malay, Chinese languages and Tamil.
fact: "Wicked" means very in New England, "y'all" means you all in the American South, and "hella" means very or lots in Northern California.
fact: Many of Australia's first settlers came from London and south-east England as well as Ireland; their mix of accents grew into the Australian accent over generations.
fact: Jamaican Patois, also called Jamaican Creole, grew from English mixed with West African languages; it is spoken by millions and has its own grammar; "Mi soon come" means I'll be there soon, though maybe later.
fact: "I couldn't care less" and the American "I could care less" both mean "I don't care at all"; "couldn't" is the original and makes more sense, so use it in careful writing.
fact: "Fixin' to" means about to in Texas and the American South.
exercise: A boy in Pittsburgh asks "Yinz want some pop?" What is he offering? | A fizzy drink to all of you | A balloon to pop for each of you | Some popcorn for one person | A ride to see his dad
exercise: A visitor from Chicago would probably call it pop, and one from California would probably call it what? | soda | juice | squash | cordial
exercise: Riley's Sydney aunt says "Grab your togs and thongs, we're going to the beach." What should Riley grab? | A swimsuit and flip-flops | A towel and some suncream lotion | A bucket and spade for sandcastles | A sun hat and sunglasses
exercise: A Kiwi boy says "Sweet as, bro, chur!" What does he mean? | Great, mate, thanks! | As sweet as what, bro? | That's too sugary, bro! | Sweets for everyone, bro!
exercise: In Cape Town a girl says "Turn left at the robot." What is the robot? | A traffic light | A machine that sweeps the street | A toy shop | A police officer in a box
exercise: A South African friend says the braai food (a braai is a barbecue) is "lekker". What does she think? | It is great and tasty | It is leaking out of the box | It is too hot to eat yet | It is a bit burnt on the edges
exercise: An Indian uncle says "Please do the needful and revert by tomorrow." What does he want? | Do what is needed and reply by tomorrow | Do something useless and come back next year | Find a needle and return it to him tomorrow | Do nothing and wait for him to phone back
exercise: What does "prepone" mean in Indian English? | To move something earlier | To put something off until much later | To cancel a meeting | To phone someone before breakfast
exercise: A Singapore hawker (a food-stall seller) says "Can lah!" What does he mean? | Yes, sure! | It is in a can. | No, not possible! | Maybe later, lah?
exercise: What has Singlish mixed with English? | Malay, Chinese languages and Tamil | Only French, from the old sailors who traded there | Only Latin, from schools | Only Spanish, from films
exercise: At camp Ollie hears "hella" in California. What does it mean? | Very, or lots | Hello, a greeting | Hot weather | Hell, a rude word
exercise: Why do Australians sound a bit like Londoners? | Many early settlers came from London and south-east England, as well as Ireland | Australians copied London accents from the radio on purpose | The British government made Australians speak like Londoners | Londoners copied Australian accents from TV soap operas
exercise: Leo's Jamaican gran says "Mi soon come." What does it mean? | I'll be there soon, maybe a bit later | I'm coming right now, this very second, wait for me | I'm not coming at all today | My son is coming instead of me
exercise: Is Jamaican Patois just bad English? | No, it grew from English and West African languages and has its own grammar | Yes, it is English spoken by people who never went to school | Yes, it is English with all the grammar taken out | No, it is a kind of Spanish
exercise: An American girl says "I could care less." What does she mean? | She doesn't care at all | She cares just a little bit | She cares a lot | She cares about less
exercise: A Texan says "He's fixin' to go to the store." What does that mean? | He's about to go to the shop | He's repairing the shop's broken door | He went shopping yesterday | He's fixing his car to drive to the shop
exercise: A South African says "I'll do it just now." When? | Some time later | This very second | Never, it is a polite no | Yesterday, already done
test: Your mate goes "all Americans talk the same, like in films, innit." What's true? | The US has many regional words and accents, like yinz, y'all, wicked and hella | He's right, every American sounds like a film star | He's right, but only in New York | Americans all speak like the Queen used to
test: An Aussie mate texts "beach this arvo, bring ya togs, it'll be heaps good." ("Heaps" means very or lots.) When and what to bring? | This afternoon, a swimsuit | This evening, some sandwiches | Tomorrow morning, some sun cream | Tonight, a big woolly jumper
test: A tourist in Johannesburg is told "Stop at the robot." She looks around for a machine. What should she look for? | A traffic light | A robot shop | A police officer | A big metal statue
test: Your Indian English cousin says "The test, set for Wednesday, has been preponed to Monday." What happened? | It was moved earlier, to Monday | It was moved later, to next week | It was cancelled completely | It was repeated on Monday and Wednesday
test: A Kiwi says "Yeah nah, sweet as, chur." What's the mood? | Friendly and relaxed: no worries, thanks | Angry and upset about something that happened | Confused and asking for help | Sad and about to cry
test: A Singapore uncle asks "Tomorrow can come or not?" What is he asking? | Can you come tomorrow? | Is it tomorrow or not? | Did you come yesterday? | Can tomorrow be cancelled?
test: What would happen if all English speakers used exactly the same words everywhere? | We'd lose words like prepone, lekker and sweet as, and the stories behind them | Nothing, because English is already exactly the same everywhere | English would split into seven new languages overnight | People in Australia would start speaking Spanish
test: A Gen Z American says "I could care less about football, ngl." ("Ngl" is not gonna lie.) Your British gran is confused. How do you explain? | It means she doesn't care at all, same as "couldn't care less" | It means she cares a little about football | It means she cares more than anyone else | It means she cares about less football
test: Your mate laughs at a Jamaican kid saying "Mi soon come." What do you tell him? | Patois is a full language with its own grammar | He's right, it's just English spoken badly by people | Patois is a made-up language for songs | Jamaicans speak only French at home
test: In a cartoon, an Aussie says "G'day mate!" and rides a kangaroo to school. What's real and what isn't? | "G'day" is a real greeting; riding kangaroos to school isn't | Everything is real, every Aussie kid rides one to school | Nothing is real, Aussies never say g'day | Kangaroo riding is real, g'day isn't
test: Puzzle: postpone means move later and prepone means move earlier. If a match is postponed from Friday by two days and then preponed by one day, when is it? | Saturday | Sunday | Thursday | Friday
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the varieties of English spoken around the world? | Each possesses distinctive vocabulary and rules of its own | All constitute defective imitations of British English | All are identical in vocabulary and pronunciation | None is recognised as a form of English
exam: Identify the meaning of the South African English word "robot" in the instruction "Turn left at the robot". | A traffic signal | A mechanical servant | A police constable | A toy shop
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the Australian accent. | It developed from the accents of settlers from London, south-east England and Ireland | It was imposed by royal decree in the nineteenth century | It was copied deliberately from American broadcasts | It has no connection with any accent of the British Isles
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the Indian English verb "prepone". | To bring forward to an earlier time | To defer to a later time, day or date | To cancel altogether | To repeat on a later date
